<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand I of Bulgaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, a German prince of the Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> family, was elected prince of Bulgaria in 1887. In 1908 he declared full independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and took the title of tsar. He led Bulgaria into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and abdicated after defeat in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-bulgaria.jpg" alt="Ferdinand I of Bulgaria" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zar_Ferdinand_Bulgarien.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferdinand was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 26 February 1861, the son of Prince August of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> and Clémentine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He was raised a Catholic in the high nobility of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and served as an officer in the Austro-Hungarian Army. The House of Saxe-Coburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> held several European thrones in the 19th century, and Ferdinand himself founded the royal dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-of-battenberg">Alexander of Battenberg</a> had abdicated in 1886 after a pro-Russian coup, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-national-assembly-of-turkey">Grand National Assembly</a> elected Ferdinand prince on 7 July 1887. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> thought him unfit, but he generally gave a good account of himself in his first two decades. After the fall of the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-stambolov">Stefan Stambolov</a> in 1894, Ferdinand had his infant son Boris converted to Orthodoxy in 1896, restoring relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and was excommunicated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a>. On 5 October 1908 he proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>'s independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and took the title of tsar, at the Church of the Holy Forty Martyrs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>.</p><p>In 1912 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and the Bulgarian army took Adrianople on 13 March 1913. Disputes over the division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-balkan-war">Second Balkan War</a>, in which Bulgaria was defeated, though the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> of 1913 gave it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-thrace">Western Thrace</a> and access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. On 11 October 1915 Bulgaria attacked Serbia after a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. After military setbacks in 1918, Ferdinand abdicated in favour of his eldest son, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-iii-of-bulgaria">Tsar Boris III</a> on 3 October 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ferdinand married Princess Marie Louise of Bourbon-Parma in 1893; she died in 1899 after the birth of their fourth child, and he married Princess Eleonore Reuss of Köstritz in 1908. After his abdication he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a>, spending time on gardening, travel and natural history. He lived to see his son Boris III die in 1943, the monarchy end in 1946 and his son Kyril executed. He died in Coburg on 10 September 1948 and was buried in the family crypt there, since the communist authorities would not let him be buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferdinand I of Bulgaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Russian anger forced Alexander's abdication in 1886, and <b>Ferdinand of Saxe-Coburg</b> was chosen in 1887, while premier Stefan Stambolov built a modern state and army.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bulgaria">Principality of Bulgaria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1917" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ferdinand I of Bulgaria"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4231 5922h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4231 5922h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1917">What was here in 1917? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
